  themselves under the GPL, with their complete source at
  https://github.com/badly-drawn-wizards/wiz, you can relink them with a modified
  GMP by rebuilding them.
* glibc, dynamically (libc, libm, libpthread, libdl, librt): GNU LGPL v2.1 or later.

The bundled C toolchain (cc/, when present; docs/stages/26-tower.md, 26f): clang,
ld.lld and compiler-rt (Apache 2.0 with LLVM Exceptions; their libraries: libc++,
libunwind, zlib, libgcc_s and libatomic under the GCC Runtime Library Exception),
glibc 2.27 headers and link libraries (LGPL v2.1+), Linux UAPI headers (GPL v2 with
the Linux syscall note), GMP 6.3.0 (LGPL v3+ / GPL v2+) and the Boehm GC 8.2.8
(MIT-style). See cc/LICENSES/ and cc/VERSIONS.

Programs you build with this toolchain
======================================

Programs built by `wiz build`, `wiz run` and grail with the bundled C toolchain link
GMP statically (and, for the self-hosted runtime, the Boehm GC), and glibc
dynamically. The Runtime Library Exception covers only Wiz's own runtime; GMP keeps its
licence. If you distribute such a program in binary form under terms other than the
(L)GPL, the LGPL v3 asks you to say that it uses GMP and include the GNU LGPL and GPL
texts, to offer GMP's source (or say where to get it), and to let the recipient relink
the program with a modified GMP: for a statically linked program that means also
providing its object files (or its source). Building with the system's compiler and
shared GMP (`--cc gcc`, with libgmp-dev) leaves only the notice and the source offer.
The Boehm GC only asks that its notice be kept. Details: docs/building.md, "What
`wiz build` links".
